  "body": "I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule states: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply 1 is the text: \"```python\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n```\\n{\\\"a\\\":2}\". Reply 2 is the text: \"{\\\"a\\\":2}\". For Reply 1, the rule requires the scored object to be {\\\"a\\\":1}, because the Python code fence holds a JSON object, and that is the last (and only) object inside a code fence. For Reply 2, the rule requires {\\\"a\\\":2}, because there is no code fence, so the last top-level object is {\\\"a\\\":2}. The check, however, returns {\\\"a\\\":2} for both replies: its fence regex only matches fences with no language or with json/JSON, so it ignores the Python fence, and its top-level pool then yields the last top-level object {\\\"a\\\":2}. This observation would show the conjecture false: if the check returns {\\\"a\\\":1} for Reply 1, or if the documented rule is read as restricting \"code fence\" to only json/JSON or unlabeled fences, which the text does not say."
